import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const page = {
  title: "How to Build a Bird Quarantine Testing Protocol",
  h1: "How to Build a Bird Quarantine Testing Protocol",
  description: "A practical quarantine protocol for new birds: separate space, risk assessment, targeted testing, monitoring, retesting and written clearance.",
  canonical: "/bird-quarantine-testing-protocol/",
  author: "SENO Avian Genetic Test",
  date: "November 8, 2026",
};
const articleHtml = "<p>Quarantine is the period when a new or returning bird is kept separate from the collection. Testing is one part of that period; it is not a substitute for separation.</p>\n<p>A good protocol answers four questions before the bird arrives: where will it stay, who will handle it, what will be tested, and what happens if the result is positive.</p>\n<h2>What a Quarantine Protocol Is</h2>\n<p>A quarantine protocol is a written sequence of actions that reduces the chance of a new bird introducing an infection. It includes physical isolation, hygiene, observation, testing, record keeping, and a clearance decision.</p>\n<p>It is not simply \"keep the bird in another room for a month.\" The details matter: airflow, footwear, shared tools, handling order, and contact with other birds.</p>\n<h2>Why Physical Isolation Is Essential</h2>\n<p>A laboratory test samples one moment. A bird may be incubating an infection, shedding intermittently, or carrying a pathogen that the chosen test does not cover. Physical separation protects the collection while those uncertainties are managed.</p>\n<p>Isolation should be real:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a separate room or building where possible;</li>\n<li>a door that is not used for the main collection;</li>\n<li>separate clothes and footwear;</li>\n<li>separate cleaning equipment;</li>\n<li>no shared food or water containers;</li>\n<li>handling the quarantined bird last, not first.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If true separation is impossible, the protocol should state the limitation and the extra precautions being used.</p>\n<h2>Preparing a Separate Quarantine Area</h2>\n<p>The area should be clean, easy to disinfect, and away from the main airspace. Remove porous materials that are hard to clean. Provide a dedicated bin for waste and a place to store samples before submission.</p>\n<p>A simple setup includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>disposable gloves;</li>\n<li>hand disinfectant;</li>\n<li>dedicated footwear or shoe covers;</li>\n<li>paper towels and a suitable disinfectant;</li>\n<li>sample collection materials;</li>\n<li>a logbook or spreadsheet.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Initial Veterinary Examination and Risk Assessment</h2>\n<p>Before testing, establish the bird's history: origin, age, previous mixing, transport, vaccination, and any signs of illness. A veterinarian should examine the bird where possible.</p>\n<p>The risk assessment should decide the target list. A single pet parrot from a known breeder is different from a group of imported pigeons or a bird returning from a show.</p>\n<h2>Choosing Tests During Quarantine</h2>\n<p>Test selection follows species, source, and clinical question. Common considerations include PBFD/BFDV and avian polyomavirus for psittacines, and pigeon-specific pathogens for Columbiformes. <em>Chlamydia psittaci</em> may be relevant where respiratory or zoonotic risk exists.</p>\n<p>The current laboratory menu and sample requirements must be confirmed. A broad panel is not automatically better than a targeted test, and a panel cannot cover pathogens that are not on its list.</p>\n<h2>Sample Collection and Submission</h2>\n<p>Use the sample type the assay requires. Feather follicles, oral swabs, cloacal swabs, and blood are not interchangeable. Collect early in the quarantine period when the protocol calls for it, and repeat only when the timing or result justifies it.</p>\n<p>Label each sample with the bird ID, species, collection date, and target. Keep the laboratory's submission instructions with the record.</p>\n<h2>Monitoring Throughout Quarantine</h2>\n<p>Observe the bird daily for changes in appetite, droppings, breathing, activity, and feather condition. Record what you see rather than relying on memory.</p>\n<p>Any new sign should prompt a veterinary review. It may also change the testing plan: a bird that develops respiratory signs may need a different sample or target than a bird that is clinically normal.</p>\n<h2>When Should Birds Be Retested?</h2>\n<p>Retesting is appropriate when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the first sample was taken too early after exposure;</li>\n<li>the pathogen is shed intermittently;</li>\n<li>the result was inconclusive or invalid;</li>\n<li>the bird developed signs;</li>\n<li>the cost of a false negative is high.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A retest should have a reason. Repeating the same test without changing timing or sample type may produce the same uncertainty.</p>\n<h2>What to Do When a Test Is Positive</h2>\n<p>A positive result means the target was detected in the sample. It is not a complete diagnosis and does not automatically define the risk to every other bird.</p>\n<p>The protocol should say who is contacted, how the bird is separated, whether confirmation is needed, and how contacts and equipment are reviewed. For a pathogen with regulatory or public-health significance, contact the relevant authority and a veterinarian.</p>\n<h2>Criteria for Ending Quarantine</h2>\n<p>A clearance decision should consider:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>the quarantine period completed;</li>\n<li>the absence of compatible clinical signs;</li>\n<li>the test results and their limits;</li>\n<li>the bird's source and exposure history;</li>\n<li>veterinary advice;</li>\n<li>the receiving collection's risk tolerance.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>There is no single test result that guarantees a bird is free of infection. The decision should be documented.</p>\n<h2>Maintaining Quarantine Records</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Record</th>\n<th>Why it matters</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Bird ID and species</td>\n<td>Ties the record to the individual</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Source and arrival date</td>\n<td>Defines the exposure history</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Quarantine start/end</td>\n<td>Shows the separation period</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Veterinary examination</td>\n<td>Records clinical findings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tests requested and sample type</td>\n<td>Shows what was actually tested</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Results and retest dates</td>\n<td>Tracks the evidence trail</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Final review</td>\n<td>Documents the clearance decision</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Laboratory Perspective</h2>\n<p>SENO provides avian molecular testing for selected pathogens. This article is a quarantine-planning guide, not a veterinary diagnosis or a guarantee that a bird is free of infection. The current test menu, sample requirements, and interpretation limits should be confirmed with the laboratory and the bird's veterinarian.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<p><strong>How long should quarantine last?</strong></p>\n<p>There is no universal duration. CDC describes at least 30 days as a general pet-bird separation example; some parrot guidance uses 30–45 days. Species, source, pathogen risk, and veterinary advice should determine the plan.</p>\n<p><strong>Can I quarantine in the same room as other birds?</strong></p>\n<p>That is not true isolation. Shared air, hands, clothing, and equipment can move pathogens. Use a separate space where possible, or document the limitation and increase hygiene.</p>\n<p><strong>Do I need to test at the start and end of quarantine?</strong></p>\n<p>That depends on the pathogen and the bird's history. Some protocols use an initial test and a retest after the exposure window; others use clinical monitoring alone. A veterinarian can set the schedule.</p>\n<p><strong>Can a negative test end quarantine early?</strong></p>\n<p>No. A negative result covers the sample and target tested. It does not replace the observation period or a risk assessment.</p>\n<p><strong>What if the bird becomes ill during quarantine?</strong></p>\n<p>Contact a veterinarian. Illness may change the sample type, target list, and urgency. Do not wait for the originally scheduled test date.</p>\n<h2>Sources</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>CDC — Psittacosis: About. https://www.cdc.gov/psittacosis/about/index.html</li>\n<li>USDA APHIS — Defend the Flock: Biosecurity. https://www.aphis.usda.gov/livestock-poultry-disease/avian/defend-the-flock</li>\n<li>Merck Veterinary Manual — Pet birds. https://www.merckvetmanual.com/exotic-and-laboratory-animals/pet-birds</li>\n<li>WOAH — Newcastle disease. https://www.woah.org/en/disease/newcastle-disease/</li>\n<li>AAVLD — Accreditation requirements. https://www.aavld.org/accreditation-requirements</li>\n<li>CDC — Bird flu: About. https://www.cdc.gov/bird-flu/about/index.html</li>\n</ol>";
